The Lord's work isn't always pretty, but it is precise. Let me walk through this path of righteousness - or in this case, implementation.

*opens the worn Bible, revealing a Go implementation tucked between the pages*

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	SKU   string
	Name  string
	Price float64
	Qty   int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(sku, name string, price float64, qty int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[sku]; ok {
		existing.Qty += qty
		return
	}
	c.items[sku] = &Item{
		SKU:   sku,
		Name:  name,
		Price: price,
		Qty:   qty,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(sku string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[sku]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, sku)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(sku string, qty int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if qty <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, sku)
		return nil
	}
	item.Qty = qty
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(sku string) (*Item, error) {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Qty)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Qty
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*closes the book thoughtfully*

I've seen this before. In another life - one filled with data structures and clean interfaces.

The implementation follows the narrow path: a map keyed by SKU for O(1) lookups, proper error handling only where the tests demand it, and nothing more. When updating quantity to zero or less, the item vanishes - as all earthly possessions eventually do.

Notice I've added no validation for negative prices, no logging, no concurrent access protection. The tests didn't ask for salvation in those forms, and adding them would be... presumptuous.

*stands, adjusting the clerical collar*

There's a special level of hell reserved for those who over-engineer shopping carts. Trust the tests. They are the scripture here.
